WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Two genetic techniques.

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.640
Bt cotton has been grown in Australian cotton

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.720
fields for about thirty years.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.400
A gene drive mosquito, built to fight malaria,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.400
has never left a lab cage.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.000
Both promise a big benefit, and both could

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.560
change biodiversity.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.840
So should they get the same verdict?

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.880
How do you weigh the benefits of a

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.680
biotechnology against its risks to biodiversity, and reach

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.360
a judgement you can defend?

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:31.286
Listing pros and cons isn't weighing.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:34.246
To weigh, put each case through the same

00:00:34.327 --> 00:00:37.206
four questions, and answer each one with evidence

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:38.566
from that case.

00:00:38.887 --> 00:00:41.126
How big is the benefit, and how strong

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.246
is the evidence for it?

00:00:42.727 --> 00:00:45.766
Which level of biodiversity changes, and compared with

00:00:45.847 --> 00:00:46.366
what?

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.566
A bigger harvest isn't a level.

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:50.806
Can the change be undone if it goes

00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:51.606
wrong?

00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:54.566
And who gains, and who carries the risk?

00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:56.926
Then finish with a verdict that has a

00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:59.766
condition attached: when is it worth it?

00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:02.726
Start with Bt cotton.

00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:06.406
The benefit is large and measured: together with

00:01:06.487 --> 00:01:09.446
better pest management, it's cut insecticide use on

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:12.646
Australian cotton by about eighty-five per cent.

00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:15.366
Compared with sprayed cotton, that can leave more

00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:18.166
insect species alive in the field, a gain

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:19.446
at the species level.

00:01:20.087 --> 00:01:23.526
Australia's native cotton species don't form fertile hybrids

00:01:23.607 --> 00:01:25.766
with it, so the gene is very unlikely

00:01:25.847 --> 00:01:27.446
to reach native plants.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.886
The main risk is caterpillars evolving resistance, a

00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:33.766
genetic change in a wild pest population, which

00:01:33.847 --> 00:01:36.046
growers slow with refuge crops.

00:01:36.327 --> 00:01:38.806
And if resistance did spread, growers could stop

00:01:38.887 --> 00:01:41.206
planting it, though they'd spray more again.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.246
Growers gain, and so do insects in and

00:01:44.327 --> 00:01:45.366
around the field.

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:47.846
Now the gene drive.

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:51.926
The need is enormous: malaria kills hundreds of

00:01:52.087 --> 00:01:54.966
thousands of people a year, mostly young children

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:55.686
in Africa.

00:01:56.327 --> 00:01:58.806
But the evidence comes from lab cages, not

00:01:58.887 --> 00:01:59.526
the wild.

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:02.246
The aim is to crash the population of

00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:05.366
one kind of malaria mosquito, out of thousands

00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:06.726
of mosquito species.

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:09.526
Whether that kind would be lost locally is

00:02:09.687 --> 00:02:12.166
uncertain, and so is the effect on animals

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:12.886
that eat them.

00:02:13.607 --> 00:02:16.326
This drive is built to spread by itself,

00:02:16.407 --> 00:02:18.406
so once it's released, it may not be

00:02:18.607 --> 00:02:19.686
possible to recall it.

00:02:20.407 --> 00:02:22.726
The people who'd gain most live where malaria

00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:25.926
is common, but a drive can cross borders,

00:02:26.007 --> 00:02:28.726
so communities and governments in many countries would

00:02:28.807 --> 00:02:29.686
need to agree.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.180
Notice which question pulls these two apart: can

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.300
it be undone?

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.100
A benefit you give up can usually be

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.100
won back.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.300
Stop growing a crop, and you can choose

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.660
something else next season.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.340
But an extinct species can't be brought back,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.100
and a drive that has spread through wild

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.980
mosquitoes could be very hard to remove.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.580
So a gain you can reverse and a

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.580
loss you can't aren't settled just by comparing

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.940
their sizes.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.260
A risk that may be permanent needs much

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.260
stronger evidence before you accept it.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:04.586
Now the verdicts.

00:03:05.147 --> 00:03:08.746
Bt cotton: to a large extent justified, provided

00:03:08.827 --> 00:03:11.026
refuges keep resistance rare.

00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:14.586
The gene drive: not yet justified for release,

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.306
but staged field trials could be, if each

00:03:17.387 --> 00:03:20.106
stage shows no serious harm and the communities

00:03:20.347 --> 00:03:21.706
affected agree.

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:22.506
Same four

00:03:23.147 --> 00:03:26.266
questions, two different verdicts, because the evidence, the

00:03:26.347 --> 00:03:29.786
reversibility and the people at risk all differ.

00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:32.666
That's why one verdict for all biotechnology is

00:03:32.827 --> 00:03:33.866
too broad.

00:03:34.187 --> 00:03:36.506
Judge each case on its evidence, and say

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.186
when your verdict would change.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.640
A good condition is one you could actually

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.480
check.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.440
For Bt cotton, the check is resistance monitoring:

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.120
each season, scientists test caterpillars from cotton regions

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.720
for resistance alleles.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.080
If those alleles became common despite the refuges,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.360
the verdict would weaken.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.920
For the gene drive, the checks would come

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.640
from each trial stage.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.000
Do the target mosquitoes fall?

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.000
Do other species hold steady?

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.760
Do the people affected agree?

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.040
Each answer could move the verdict either way.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:12.786
Two things to hold on to.

00:04:13.347 --> 00:04:16.626
A benefit can be paused; an extinction can't

00:04:16.707 --> 00:04:17.426
be undone.

00:04:17.987 --> 00:04:21.026
That's why reversibility weighs so heavily.

00:04:21.347 --> 00:04:24.226
And an evaluation isn't finished until it ends

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:27.906
in a verdict, with "provided that": the condition

00:04:27.987 --> 00:04:29.266
under which it holds.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:31.906
Two exam questions now.

00:04:32.387 --> 00:04:32.866
First.

00:04:33.507 --> 00:04:36.786
Some mosquito control programs release males carrying a

00:04:36.867 --> 00:04:39.986
gene that kills daughters before they become adults.

00:04:40.627 --> 00:04:43.426
Sons who inherit it survive, and each passes

00:04:43.507 --> 00:04:45.506
it to about half his own offspring.

00:04:46.147 --> 00:04:48.506
Explain why the gene should disappear from the

00:04:48.547 --> 00:04:51.986
wild population once releases stop, and why stopping

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:55.826
releases alone may not remove a self-sustaining suppression

00:04:55.907 --> 00:04:56.986
gene drive.

00:04:57.347 --> 00:04:58.706
Pause here to answer.

00:05:09.134 --> 00:05:12.253
Without new releases, each carrier son passes the

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:15.053
gene to only half his offspring, and every

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:17.453
daughter who inherits it dies.

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:20.173
So selection removes it, and it gets rarer

00:05:20.254 --> 00:05:22.813
each generation until it's lost.

00:05:22.894 --> 00:05:26.093
A drive designed to bias inheritance above one-half

00:05:26.214 --> 00:05:29.213
can keep spreading after releases stop; its spread

00:05:29.254 --> 00:05:31.613
in the wild is still untested.

00:05:31.654 --> 00:05:34.493
Saying that stopping releases stops any gene drive

00:05:34.574 --> 00:05:36.093
is the classic mistake.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:37.860
Second.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.460
A region's farmers grow many local wheat varieties.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.300
A modified, drought-tolerant wheat has out-yielded them in

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.180
dry-year trials, and a wild grass that can

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.740
cross with wheat grows along the field edges.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.940
It's proposed that the new wheat replace all

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:55.220
the local varieties.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.060
Evaluate this proposal, weighing its benefits against its

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.820
effects on biodiversity.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.820
Pause here to answer.

00:06:13.234 --> 00:06:17.073
The benefit: steadier drought harvests, though shown only

00:06:17.154 --> 00:06:18.513
in dry-year trials.

00:06:19.074 --> 00:06:22.273
But one variety replacing twenty narrows the crop's

00:06:22.354 --> 00:06:25.713
genetic diversity, and lost ones may never return.

00:06:26.274 --> 00:06:28.273
The gene could also spread into the wild

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:28.993
grass.

00:06:29.474 --> 00:06:32.033
So replacing them all isn't justified.

00:06:32.594 --> 00:06:35.473
Growing it could be, provided local varieties are

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:38.033
kept and it's planted away from the grass.

00:06:38.674 --> 00:06:41.153
No verdict is the classic mistake.

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:44.446
So when a question asks you to weigh

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:47.486
a biotechnology, put each case through the same

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:52.046
four questions: benefit and evidence, level and baseline,

00:06:52.127 --> 00:06:54.926
can it be undone, who gains and who

00:06:55.007 --> 00:06:55.966
carries the risk.

00:06:56.447 --> 00:06:59.086
Then end with a verdict and its condition,

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:01.646
and make sure that condition is something evidence

00:07:01.727 --> 00:07:02.806
could check.

00:07:03.327 --> 00:07:08.286
Next, how artificial insemination and artificial pollination compare.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.940
Here are the key notes.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.100
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:13.980
to copy them.
